<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <parent>
        <groupId>hr.hrg.jcodebuddy</groupId>
        <artifactId>jcodebuddy-parent</artifactId>
        <version>1.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
        <relativePath>../pom.xml</relativePath>
    </parent>

    <artifactId>jcodebuddy-core</artifactId>
    <packaging>jar</packaging>

    <name>JCodeBuddy Core</name>
    <description>
        Generated-code markers and the parser that finds generated regions in a file, for any tool that
        needs to know which parts of a source file a generator owns. No dependencies, so a linter, a
        migration tool, an IDE inspection or an agent can use it without pulling in a generator.
    </description>

    <!--
        WHY THIS MODULE EXISTS, AND WHY IT HAS NO DEPENDENCIES.

        Generated code is committed source and it is cooperative: a member from an earlier revision
        survives regeneration, so a file can be partly generated and partly hand-written, and the boundary
        used to be knowable only to the generator — because the recogniser WAS the generator. Every other
        consumer (an external static parser, an AI agent reading the source, a reviewer reading a diff)
        needs that boundary and has no access to a generator's internals. DEC-035 fixes the marker
        vocabulary; this module is the parser that reads it.

        It is a separate module from `hipster-entity-tooling`, where the emitters live, for one reason:
        what a consumer gets. `hipster-entity-tooling` carries the OpenRewrite LST, Jackson and the entity
        generator's whole model, and a tool that only wants to know where generated code stops should not
        have to resolve any of that. This module is a leaf: no dependencies, no generator types, nothing to
        configure. A consumer reads source text and gets line spans back.

        That also makes the direction of the dependency the honest one. The generator depends on the
        vocabulary (it emits markers this parser understands); the parser must never depend on the
        generator, or it could not be used by a project that does not use this one.
    -->

    <dependencies>
        <!-- The one dependency, and only for tests: the parser itself is pure JDK, which is what lets a
             consumer add this module without resolving a generator, a JSON library or a parser library. -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.junit.jupiter</groupId>
            <artifactId>junit-jupiter</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
    </dependencies>
</project>
